            "The World Health Organization defines <strong>health</strong> as a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity — an ideal rather than a destination, which is why nurses think of a <strong>health–illness continuum</strong> along which every person moves, from high-level wellness through ordinary good health, through symptoms and disability, to critical illness and death. <strong>Disease</strong> is the pathology a doctor names; <strong>illness</strong> is the person's experience of being unwell, which may exist without disease and be absent despite it. A woman with well-controlled hypertension is not ill; a man convinced that a curse is destroying him is ill in every way that matters to his nursing, whatever his investigations show. Health is shaped by <strong>determinants</strong> in layers: the age, sex and genes a person is born with; the lifestyle they choose within their means; the family, neighbourhood and church or mosque that support or stigmatise them; the water, sanitation, housing, food, education, work and services they can reach; and the economy, culture, environment and laws that frame everything — which is why health promotion that addresses only behaviour so often fails.",
