            "<strong>Valid consent</strong> has three elements. The person has <strong>capacity</strong> — can understand the information, retain it long enough to decide, weigh it and communicate a decision — and capacity is <strong>presumed</strong> in every adult until shown otherwise, so a diagnosis of mental illness, old age, illiteracy or a decision you disagree with does not remove it. They have been given the <strong>information</strong> a reasonable person would want — what is proposed and why, its benefits and risks, the alternatives including doing nothing, and the consequences of refusal — in a language and at a level they follow. And the decision is <strong>voluntary</strong>, free of pressure from staff, husband, mother-in-law or pastor, and may be withdrawn at any moment, including after the form is signed and the trolley is moving. Consent may be <strong>implied</strong> (the arm held out for the blood pressure cuff), <strong>verbal</strong> (most nursing procedures) or <strong>written</strong> (surgery, anaesthesia, transfusion, HIV testing, research), and the written form is only the record of a conversation. Obtaining consent for an operation is the doctor's duty; the nurse's duty is to witness the signature, confirm that the patient understood, and stop the list if they did not. In a <strong>true emergency</strong>, where an unconscious patient will die or suffer serious harm without immediate treatment and no one with authority is available, treatment proceeds in the patient's best interests and is documented as such. For a <strong>child</strong>, a parent or guardian consents, the child's own assent is sought as far as their understanding allows, and a parent's refusal of life-saving treatment can be overridden in the child's best interests. A relative <strong>cannot</strong> consent for a competent adult, and treatment without consent is an assault in law even when it succeeds.",
