            "The <strong>nursing process</strong> is the systematic, patient-centred method by which nurses identify what a person needs and provide it: <strong>assess</strong> — collect the data; <strong>diagnose</strong> — name the problem the nurse can treat; <strong>plan</strong> — set goals and choose interventions; <strong>implement</strong> — carry them out; <strong>evaluate</strong> — ask whether the goal was met, and go round again. It is written as five steps but practised as a <strong>cycle</strong>, because the evaluation of one plan is the assessment for the next, and because each step depends on the one before: skip assessment and the diagnosis is a guess, skip the goal and evaluation has nothing to measure. Its virtues are the virtues of any scientific method — it is <strong>systematic</strong>, so nothing is left to chance; <strong>dynamic</strong>, so it changes as the patient changes; <strong>patient-centred</strong>, so the goals belong to the person, not to the ward; <strong>outcome-focused</strong>, so it can be shown to have worked; and <strong>universal</strong>, so the same thinking serves a labouring woman, a child with pneumonia and a man dying at home.",
