            "An <strong>incident</strong> is any event that caused or could have caused harm to a patient, visitor or staff member — a fall, a drug error, a pressure injury acquired on the ward, a needlestick, an assault, a missing patient, equipment failure, a specimen mislabelled. A <strong>near miss</strong> is the same event caught before it reached the patient — the wrong drug drawn up and noticed at the bedside check. Both are <strong>reported</strong> on the facility's incident form, factually, promptly and by the person who found or made the error, after the patient has first been made safe and the doctor informed. The report is not the nursing record: the record states what happened to the patient and what was done, in the ordinary way, while the incident form goes to the quality system; the record does not say 'incident form completed'. The patient and, where appropriate, the family are <strong>told the truth</strong> about an error that affected them, with an apology and an explanation of what will be done — veracity after an error is a professional duty, and concealment turns a mistake into misconduct.",
            "What happens next decides whether the next nurse makes the same error. In a <strong>blame culture</strong> the question is 'who did this?'; the nurse is disciplined and told to be more careful, everyone learns that reporting costs you something, near misses stop being reported, the look-alike labels and the chronic short-staffing that allowed the error stay exactly as they were, and errors do not fall — they go quiet. In a <strong>just culture</strong> the question is 'what allowed this?'; the system is examined — the interruptions during the drug round, the two vials that look identical, the illegible prescription, the handover that was skipped — and changed, and the change is checked later; honest errors are treated as information, repeated carelessness is addressed with support and supervision, and reckless or deliberate acts are still dealt with individually. A rise in reported incidents after a just culture is introduced is a sign of safety, not of decline. The nurse's part is to report her own errors and near misses, to report an <strong>unsafe situation</strong> — a staffing level, a broken suction, a colleague who is impaired — through the line manager and in writing, and never to be the person who knew and said nothing.",
